---
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import { path } from '../lib/paths';
import { SITE } from '../lib/site';
---

<Page
  title="Privacy"
  description="What MathemAct stores, who processes it, and how to have it erased."
  lead="What is stored, who else sees it, and how to get rid of it. In plain terms, because a privacy notice nobody reads protects nobody."
  updated="August 2026"
>
  <p class="status">
    Accounts are built but not yet open, so nothing described here is being collected
    today. This notice is published in advance so that it can be read before anyone signs
    up rather than after.
  </p>

  <h2>What is stored</h2>

  <dl class="items">
    <dt>Your email address</dt>
    <dd>
      Needed to confirm the account, to sign you in, and to reset a password. It is also
      what your institutional badge is derived from. It is <strong>never displayed</strong>
      on the site, never returned by the API, never included in the public export, and
      never shown to moderators.
    </dd>

    <dt>Your display name</dt>
    <dd>
      Shown on everything you post. It may be a pseudonym, at every verification tier.
    </dd>

    <dt>Your institution, if the domain matched</dt>
    <dd>
      Derived automatically from your confirmed email domain against the Research
      Organization Registry, along with the date of that check. Stored as the institution
      name and country. You cannot set or edit this yourself.
    </dd>

    <dt>What you post</dt>
    <dd>
      Practices, comments, ratings, and still-works confirmations, with the date each was
      made. Ratings are stored per person so that you can change yours; individual ratings
      are never shown attributed to a name.
    </dd>

    <dt>An erasure request, if you make one</dt>
    <dd>
      The fact that your account has asked to be erased, when it was asked, and anything
      you chose to add. Only you and an administrator can see it, and withdrawing the
      request deletes the record of it outright.
    </dd>
  </dl>

  <h3>What is not stored</h3>

  <p>
    There is no analytics of any kind, no advertising, no tracking pixels, and no
    fingerprinting. Nothing about your visit is recorded while you read: the pages are
    static files, and the database is contacted only when you sign in or post something.
    Fonts are served from this site rather than from anyone else's font service.
  </p>

  <p>
    Exactly one script is loaded from another company, on exactly four pages: Cloudflare's
    Turnstile check, where you sign up, sign in, reset a password, or ask for another
    confirmation link. It is not on any page you only read, and you can confirm that by
    viewing source.
  </p>

  <p>
    There is no cookie banner because there are no cookies. Being signed in is kept in this
    browser's own local storage, written when you sign in and removed when you sign out.
  </p>

  <h2>Who processes it</h2>

  <p>Four services are involved. Each receives only what it needs to do its job.</p>

  <dl class="items">
    <dt>Supabase</dt>
    <dd>
      Database and authentication, hosted in the European Union. Holds everything listed
      above. This is the only processor that sees the whole picture.
    </dd>

    <dt>Brevo</dt>
    <dd>
      Sends the confirmation and password-reset emails, and therefore receives your email
      address and the contents of those messages. Nothing else. It is never used to send
      announcements.
    </dd>

    <dt>Cloudflare</dt>
    <dd>
      Provides the Turnstile check that keeps automated signups out. It receives your IP
      address and a token when you sign up, sign in, ask for a password reset, or ask for
      another confirmation link. Its script is loaded on those four pages and nowhere else,
      so it is never involved in a page you only read.
    </dd>

    <dt>GitHub</dt>
    <dd>
      Serves the site's files, and therefore receives your IP address and browser user
      agent as part of any ordinary web request. This is unavoidable for any website; no
      account is needed to read this one.
    </dd>

  </dl>

  <h2>What is public</h2>

  <p>
    Everything you post is public, and is published under a
    <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/" rel="license">CC BY 4.0</a>
    licence. It is also included in a nightly export that anyone can download in full. Assume
    that anything you post can be copied, archived, quoted, and analysed by people you did not
    anticipate, because it can. Your email address is not in the export.
  </p>

  <p>
    Take particular care with transcript excerpts. Remove anyone else's unpublished
    material before you paste, and remember that a transcript may contain more identifying
    detail than you noticed when you were in the conversation.
  </p>

  <h2>Erasing your account</h2>

  <p>
    Ask on the <a href={path('/account/delete/')}>erasure page</a> while signed in, and it
    will be done. Your profile, email address, and institutional record are deleted
    outright. Asking from within the account means nobody has to prove who they are by
    email, and it means the request cannot be made by someone else on your behalf. If you
    cannot sign in, write to <a href={`mailto:${SITE.contactEmail}`}>{SITE.contactEmail}</a
    > from the address the account uses.
  </p>

  <p>
    Content you posted is detached from you rather than deleted with you: the body of a
    practice or comment is removed and the author attribution is stripped, but the thread
    structure stays, so that the discussion around it does not collapse into
    incomprehensibility. If you want specific posts deleted as well, say so and they will
    be. Anything already downloaded by someone else from a previous export cannot be
    recalled, which is true of anything published anywhere.
  </p>

  <h2>Your rights</h2>

  <p>
    If you are in the European Union or the United Kingdom you have the right to see what
    is held about you, to have it corrected, to have it erased, to get a copy in a portable
    form, and to object to how it is used. Write to
    <a href={`mailto:${SITE.contactEmail}`}>{SITE.contactEmail}</a> and it will be dealt
    with. If a request is refused you will be told why, and you can complain to your
    national data protection authority.
  </p>

  <h2>Changes</h2>

  <p>
    If this notice changes in a way that affects what is collected or who sees it,
    registered users will be told before it takes effect, not after. The date at the top
    is the date of the last change, and the full history is in the
    <a href={SITE.repository}>public repository</a>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Questions about any of this, including the awkward ones, go to
    <a href={`mailto:${SITE.contactEmail}`}>{SITE.contactEmail}</a>. The
    <a href={path('/about/')}>about page</a> explains how verification works in more detail.
  </p>
</Page>

<style>
  .status {
    padding: var(--space-sm);
    border: var(--border);
    background-color: var(--surface);
    font-size: var(--size-2);
  }

  .items {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .items dt {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-md);
    font-weight: var(--weight-semibold);
  }

  .items dd {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-3xs);
    margin-inline-start: 0;
  }
</style>
